{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789397646,"nonce":"oL8mGi2EjPL49293bnNMX9Uitaw=","title":"Python: a closure made in a loop is never released","message":"pressure/closures.py, `f = lambda x: x + i; total += apply(f, 1)` per\nstep, grows by about 270 bytes a step (30 MB at 2,000 steps, 82 MB at\n200,000).\n\nPer step: a box for `i` into its cell, the function record (a\n`List\u003cAny\u003e` header, its buffer, and boxes for the code address and\narity), and with `apply` inlined the dynamic call builds an argument\nlist (header, buffer, two boxes). The record is handed to the closure's\nown code as its environment through an indirect call, which the drop\npass has to read as an escape, and every box lands in a list buffer,\nwhich is an escape too: a list does not own its elements, since the\nsame box may sit in two lists.\n\nWhat would fix it: a known-callee direct call for a lambda called\nthrough a typed local (the record never needs to exist), and for the\ngeneral case either ownership of boxes by the containers they are\nstored into once (needs alias facts) or a collector (b94b4cb). Related:\ne132f21, b1e6272.","files":null}]}